/P 100 0 R
/T (Origins of the World Trade Organization \(WTO\))
/E (Origins of the World Trade Organization \(WTO\))
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 100 0 R
/T (The institutional foundations of the World Trade Organization \(WTO\) lie in the post-World War II global effort to reconstruct international economic governance and prevent the severe economic protectionism that had exacerbated the Great Depression. At the Bretton Woods Conference in 1944, international leaders intended to create three pillars for global governance: the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank, and the International Trade Organization \(ITO\). Although fifty-three nations drafted the Havana Charter in 1948 to establish the ITO, the organization failed to materialize after the United States Congress refused to ratify the agreement, citing concerns over infringements on domestic economic sovereignty. )
/E (The institutional foundations of the World Trade Organization \(WTO\) lie in the post-World War II global effort to reconstruct international economic governance and prevent the severe economic protectionism that had exacerbated the Great Depression. At the Bretton Woods Conference in 1944, international leaders intended to create three pillars for global governance: the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank, and the International Trade Organization \(ITO\). Although fifty-three nations drafted the Havana Charter in 1948 to establish the ITO, the organization failed to materialize after the United States Congress refused to ratify the agreement, citing concerns over infringements on domestic economic sovereignty. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 100 0 R
/T (In the absence of a permanent international trade organization, nations relied on the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade \(GATT\), signed in 1947 by twenty-three founding countries as a provisional, temporary legal treaty. Over nearly five decades, GATT operated without a formal institutional headquarters or permanent status, successfully lowering industrial tariffs across eight successive negotiating rounds. However, GATT developed significant structural weaknesses over time. Its regulatory framework was strictly limited to physical merchandise trade, entirely excluding modern economic sectors such as services, cross-border investment, and intellectual property protection. Additionally, GATT permitted voluntary plurilateral codes that fragmented trade obligations and lacked a binding judicial mechanism, as dispute settlement required total consensus among members, effectively allowing defendant nations to block adverse legal rulings. )
/E (In the absence of a permanent international trade organization, nations relied on the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade \(GATT\), signed in 1947 by twenty-three founding countries as a provisional, temporary legal treaty. Over nearly five decades, GATT operated without a formal institutional headquarters or permanent status, successfully lowering industrial tariffs across eight successive negotiating rounds. However, GATT developed significant structural weaknesses over time. Its regulatory framework was strictly limited to physical merchandise trade, entirely excluding modern economic sectors such as services, cross-border investment, and intellectual property protection. Additionally, GATT permitted voluntary plurilateral codes that fragmented trade obligations and lacked a binding judicial mechanism, as dispute settlement required total consensus among members, effectively allowing defendant nations to block adverse legal rulings. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 100 0 R
/T (To overcome these institutional vulnerabilities, 123 participating nations launched the Uruguay Round in 1986, initiating eight years of comprehensive trade negotiations aimed at modernizing international commercial law. The Uruguay Round culminated in April 1994 with the signing of the Marrakesh Agreement in Morocco, which formally established the World Trade Organization as a permanent, fully autonomous international body headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland. Coming into force on January 1, 1995, the WTO absorbed GATT 1947 into an expanded regulatory architecture governed by a "single undertaking" principle, requiring member states to accept the complete body of WTO agreements, including new governance over cross-border services \(GATS\), trade-related intellectual property rights \(TRIPS\), and investment measures \(TRIMS\).)
/E (To overcome these institutional vulnerabilities, 123 participating nations launched the Uruguay Round in 1986, initiating eight years of comprehensive trade negotiations aimed at modernizing international commercial law. The Uruguay Round culminated in April 1994 with the signing of the Marrakesh Agreement in Morocco, which formally established the World Trade Organization as a permanent, fully autonomous international body headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland. Coming into force on January 1, 1995, the WTO absorbed GATT 1947 into an expanded regulatory architecture governed by a "single undertaking" principle, requiring member states to accept the complete body of WTO agreements, including new governance over cross-border services \(GATS\), trade-related intellectual property rights \(TRIPS\), and investment measures \(TRIMS\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
