309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 104 0 R
/E (The legal architecture governing digital trade within the WTO consists of a hybrid matrix of historical multilateral treaties, temporary ministerial mandates, and modern plurilateral agreements: )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 776 0 R
/E (1998 Work Programme on Electronic Commerce & The Moratorium: Established at the 2nd Ministerial Conference, this foundational directive tasked WTO councils with examining all trade-related aspects of e-commerce across existing treaties. A primary component of this mandate was the temporary E-Commerce Moratorium, an agreement in which member states pledged not to impose customs duties on electronic transmissions. Periodically extended at subsequent Ministerial Conferences, the moratorium's status has become a major flashpoint in global trade negotiations. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 777 0 R
/E (GATT 1994 \(General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade\): Governs physical goods ordered or facilitated via digital networks \(such as cross-border ecommerce fulfillment\). GATT provisions regulate physical border procedures, customs valuation, tariff schedules, and non-discriminatory treatment once physical packages cross international borders. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 778 0 R
/E (GATS \(General Agreement on Trade in Services\): Serves as the primary legal foundation for "digitally delivered services" \(e.g., cloud computing, financial services, software-as-a-service, and remote consulting\). Modern digital communications primarily operate under Mode 1 \(Cross-Border Supply\) and Mode 2 \(Consumption Abroad\) of GATS. Digital service flows are governed by members' specific schedule commitments, Most-Favoured-Nation \(MFN\) obligations, and National Treatment rules. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 779 0 R
/E (TRIPS \(Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights\): Protects foundational intangible assets of the digital economy. TRIPS sets baseline international protection standards for computer software code, algorithms, digital copyrights, technology patents, and commercial trade secrets across member jurisdictions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 780 0 R
/E (Plurilateral Joint Statement Initiative \(JSI\) on E-Commerce: Because full multilateral consensus stalled under traditional WTO procedures, a coalition of over 80 member nations negotiated a dedicated Plurilateral Agreement on Electronic Commerce. The JSI text establishes shared rules for e-signatures, paperless trade administration, spam protection, open government data, and digital consumer rights, while seeking common ground on data transfers and proprietary source code protection. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
