1506 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the absence of a permanent international trade organization, nations relied on the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade \(GATT\), signed in 1947 by twenty-three founding countries as a provisional, temporary legal treaty. Over nearly five decades, GATT operated without a formal institutional headquarters or permanent status, successfully lowering industrial tariffs across eight successive negotiating rounds. However, GATT developed significant structural weaknesses over time. Its regulatory framework was strictly limited to physical merchandise trade, entirely excluding modern economic sectors such as services, cross-border investment, and intellectual property protection. Additionally, GATT permitted voluntary plurilateral codes that fragmented trade obligations and lacked a binding judicial mechanism, as dispute settlement required total consensus among members, effectively allowing defendant nations to block adverse legal rulings.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1485 0 R
/Prev 1497 0 R
/Next 1507 0 R
>>
endobj
1507 0 obj
<<
/Title (To overcome these institutional vulnerabilities, 123 participating nations launched the Uruguay Round in 1986, initiating eight years of comprehensive trade negotiations aimed at modernizing international commercial law. The Uruguay Round culminated in April 1994 with the signing of the Marrakesh Agreement in Morocco, which formally established the World Trade Organization as a permanent, fully autonomous international body headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland. Coming into force on January 1, 1995, the WTO absorbed GATT 1947 into an expanded regulatory architecture governed by a "single undertaking" principle, requiring member states to accept the complete body of WTO agreements, including new governance over cross-border services \(GATS\), trade-related intellectual property rights \(TRIPS\), and investment measures \(TRIMS\).)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1485 0 R
/Prev 1506 0 R
/Next 1498 0 R
>>
endobj
1508 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1469 0 R
/Prev 1499 0 R
/Next 1509 0 R
>>
endobj
1509 0 obj
<<
/Title (Third, the WTO holds a unique mandate to resolve commercial disputes among sovereign states through its Dispute Settlement Body \(DSB\). Designed to replace political power plays with a legal framework, the DSB operates independent panels to interpret treaty provisions, issue binding judicial rulings, and authorize proportionate retaliatory trade measures if a non-compliant state fails to align its national legislation with WTO obligations.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1469 0 R
/Prev 1508 0 R
/Next 1500 0 R
>>
endobj
